{
  "id": "COMPOUND:1139 - DISEASE:1932",
  "verbalization": "Halothane, a volatile anesthetic, appears to influence the coiled-coil domain containing 33 (CCD33) protein, which is implicated in multiple pathophysiological conditions. The evidence suggests that halothane may act through a shared mechanism involving CCD33, leading to the development of Macrostomia. This is supported by the observed causal relationships between halothane and CCD33, which in turn are linked to Macrostomia in both Hypophosphatemic rickets, X-linked dominant and Melanoma-Pancreatic cancer syndrome. The consistent involvement of CCD33 in these conditions, coupled with the direct effect of halothane on this protein, implies a potential mechanistic link between halothane and Macrostomia. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of CCD33 in regulating developmental and pathological processes, suggesting that halothane may exert its effects through a common biological mechanism that impacts CCD33 and subsequently contributes to the manifestation of Macrostomia.",
